id: pm-gh1539
title: "GH-1539: enforce command builder keys and preserve literal types"
description: "Reported against published2026.10.21: defineCommand accepts descripton/read_ouput and widens name to string under strict TypeScript5.9. Source https://github.com/unbraind/pm-cli/issues/1539 includes a public-package reproduction. Current src/sdk/define.ts still uses an ordinary generic identity builder while its docs promise literal preservation. The closed original feature shipped the builder and remains completed; this is a new exactness/literal-contract report requiring public type and contextual-handler checks."
type: Issue
status: closed
priority: 1
tags[3]: agent-ux,github-report,sdk
created_at: "2026-10-21T21:28:05.779Z"
updated_at: "2026-10-22T01:06:49.364Z"
closed_at: "2026-10-22T01:06:48.428Z"
completed_at: "2026-10-22T01:06:48.428Z"
author: "harness:codex"
estimated_minutes: 120
acceptance_criteria: Reject misspelled keys in fresh public command definitions; Preserve literal name/action plus contextual handler inference; Audit sibling object builders under the same advertised contract; Verify public package declarations and legitimate customization compatibility
parent: pm-ugqx
risk: medium
confidence: 80
severity: medium
resolution: "All object and scoped-object authoring builders use const generic inference; command, manifest and profile definitions enforce exact top-level keys. Flag builders expose supported repeatable and value_validation metadata while callbacks keep contextual typing."
expected_result: "Public builders preserve literal names/actions and callback inference, reject misspelled keys, and support documented registration metadata with unchanged runtime identity."
actual_result: "Public aggregate and authoring compile fixtures cover all object/scoped builders, fresh and separately authored typo inputs, supported flag metadata and callback argument typing. Packed TypeScript 5.9.3 passes, degraded installed declarations fail as intended, restoration passes, and Node/Bun runtime identity and activation pass. Full coverage is 100 percent."
